---
import { getCollection } from "astro:content"
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components"
import Eyebrow from "@/components/ui/Eyebrow.astro"
import { learnParts, starterCommands } from "@/features/learn/course"
import LessonCode from "@/features/learn/LessonCode.astro"
import { lessonLabel, lessonRoutes } from "@/features/learn/routes"
import PageLayout from "@/layouts/PageLayout.astro"

export const prerender = true

const routes = lessonRoutes(await getCollection("learn"))
const parts = learnParts.flatMap((part) => {
  const lessons = routes.filter((route) => route.entry.data.part === part.part)
  // Guides such as setup are budgeted on their own, not in their part.
  const minutes = lessons.reduce(
    (total, { entry: { data } }) => total + (data.mode === "guide" ? 0 : data.minutes),
    0,
  )
  return lessons.length > 0 ? [{ ...part, lessons, minutes }] : []
})
// The course budget: every lesson, plus the guides that are budgeted, such as
// setup. Best case, with nothing optional.
const total = routes.reduce((sum, { entry: { data } }) => sum + (data.minutes ?? 0), 0)
const hours = Math.floor(total / 60)
---

<PageLayout
  title="Learn Effect | Build reflex"
  description="Learn Effect by building reflex, a small application that checks expenses, watches services, and sends alerts. Start in your browser with no Effect experience needed."
  themeable
>
  <div class="mx-auto flex max-w-3xl flex-col gap-12 px-4 py-12 md:py-20">
    <header>
      <Eyebrow class="mb-3">Learn Effect</Eyebrow>
      <h1 class="text-3xl font-semibold text-foreground md:text-4xl">Build reflex</h1>
      <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-muted-foreground">
        Some things are worth keeping an eye on, but not checking by hand all day. An unusually
        large expense, perhaps, or a service that's gone down. In this course, we'll build reflex, a
        small application that watches for those situations and acts on rules you give it. We'll
        start with a single alert in your browser and grow it into a server you can use from the
        command line, learning Effect as we need it. If you're comfortable with TypeScript, you're
        ready to begin. No prior Effect experience needed.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-subtle-foreground">
        Plan for at least {hours} h {total - hours * 60} min ({total} minutes), including setup. That's
        the best-case lesson time; leave room for optional exercises and troubleshooting.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section aria-labelledby="finished-app" class="flex flex-col gap-4">
      <h2 id="finished-app" class="text-2xl font-semibold text-foreground">
        A session with the finished app
      </h2>
      <p class="text-prose-foreground">
        Here's where we're headed. In this session from the finished reference solution, reflex
        handles expenses and checks a service's status. We use a local HTTP receiver, a small server
        on port 4600 that lets us try rules without calling outside services. One rule sends it a
        request for every expense event, while another repeatedly checks its
        <code>/status</code> page.
      </p>
      <LessonCode file="checkpoints/24/transcript.txt" />
      <p class="text-prose-foreground">
        You don't need to understand every command yet. Notice the <code>runs list</code> output: reflex
        records what happened, including a reason when a run doesn't succeed. Later, we'll also look at
        a trace, a view of the work behind an <code>events submit</code> or
        <code>rules run</code> command. It lets us follow the request from the CLI through the server
        to each rule. We'll set up that viewer together in Part 2.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="before-you-start" class="flex flex-col gap-4">
      <h2 id="before-you-start" class="text-2xl font-semibold text-foreground">Before you start</h2>
      <p class="text-prose-foreground">
        Part 1 runs in your browser, so there's nothing to install before the first lesson. When we
        move to your machine in Part 2, you'll need:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 text-prose-foreground">
        <li>Node 22.16 or newer</li>
        <li>pnpm, or corepack to provide it</li>
        <li>git and a browser</li>
        <li>macOS, Linux with glibc 2.39 or newer, or Docker, for the trace viewer</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="text-prose-foreground">
        We'll use a starter project for the rest of the course. You can leave these commands until
        Part 2; <a href="/learn/part-2/setup" class="underline">Setting up your machine</a>
        will walk you through them when you get there.
      </p>
      <Code code={starterCommands} lang="sh" meta="showLineNumbers=false" />
    </section>

    {
      parts.map(({ part, title, venue, lessons, minutes }) => (
        <section aria-labelledby={`part-${part}`} class="flex flex-col gap-4">
          <div>
            <Eyebrow class="mb-1 text-xs">
              Part {part} · {venue} · {minutes} min
            </Eyebrow>
            <h2 id={`part-${part}`} class="text-2xl font-semibold text-foreground">
              {title}
            </h2>
          </div>
          <ol class="flex flex-col divide-y divide-border rounded-lg border border-border">
            {lessons.map(({ entry, slug }) => (
              <li>
                <a
                  href={`/learn/${slug}`}
                  class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-4 px-4 py-3 text-muted-foreground transition-colors hover:bg-muted hover:text-foreground"
                >
                  <span>{lessonLabel(entry)}</span>
                  {entry.data.minutes !== undefined && (
                    <span class="shrink-0 text-sm text-subtle-foreground">
                      {entry.data.minutes} min
                    </span>
                  )}
                </a>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ol>
        </section>
      ))
    }
  </div>
</PageLayout>
